    872:| §P.P1 batched-on-modulus q amortisation, slot 1 — per-(q, a) explicit-formula evaluator with three-axis decoupled cost (zero-database setup ⊥ per-χ partial-sum eval ⊥ orthogonal χ̄(a) sum): does the zero database admit cross-character sharing for χ of the same conductor q, and is per-character evaluation cost x-independent? | REFINEMENT (architecture + empirical baseline) | I (slot-1 baseline; slot 2-5 attack the χ-amortisation question) | Thread 6 commit step 1/5 (S227). Built `experiments/analytic/batched_q_amortisation/per_q_a_explicit_formula_profile.py` — three-axis decoupled evaluator: `time_zero_db_setup(q, K)` (per-character cache build), `time_per_chi_eval(zeros, x)` (vectorised numpy partial sum Σ_ρ x^ρ/ρ for one χ at one x), `time_orthogonality(q)` (χ̄(a) · ψ_χ vector dot of length φ(q)). Profiled at q ∈ {101, 1009, 10007} for x ∈ {1e6, 1e8} and K ∈ {50, 200, 800}. Synthetic non-principal-χ zeros via Riemann-von Mangoldt density inversion (numpy-vectorised Newton); principal-χ zeros from cached ζ-zeros. **Q1 — T_zero_db_setup shape**: linear in φ(q)·K with constant ~2.7µs per (χ, K-zero), held to within 5% across three decades of q. **No naive cross-character zero sharing** at the database level — zeros for distinct χ of the same conductor are structurally independent (block-GUE statistics with O(1/T) cross-character correlation, asymptotically negligible at K = 800 / x = 10⁶). **Q2 — T_per_chi_eval x-dependence**: x-independent within measurement noise (10⁶ vs 10⁸ identical to µs); ~50ns per zero asymptotically (numpy K=800 vectorised). Same scaling as Thread 5 ζ-zero primitive. **Q3 — Orthogonality vs evaluation**: orthogonality is 2-4 orders of magnitude subdominant for q ≤ 10⁴ (3.8 µs at q=101, 336 µs at q=10007 vs full_per_x of 1.9 ms / 268 ms respectively). Per-(q, a) cost is dominated by φ(q)·T_per_chi_eval. **a-direction batching amortisation**: at q=1009, K=200, x=10⁶, varying a with M ∈ {1, 4, 16, 64, 256}: T_amort/a goes 188 → 64 → 33 → 25 → 23 ms, **8.06× speedup at M=256** (asymptote = T_full_per_x = 22.7ms, independent of M by construction since zeros are a-independent). **This is the trivial amortisable direction** — the zero database is reused across a-queries with no extra work. The non-trivial χ-direction is slot 2's target. **Falsifier list (F1-F3) framing slots 2-5**: (F1) Bober-Hiary 2017 / Booker-Lobb 2009 multi-character L-zero algorithm sub-linear in φ(q) within single conductor; (F2) AFE-shared partial-sum evaluation `L(½+it, χ) ≈ Σ_n χ(n)/n^{½+it} + ...` at fixed t across all χ mod q (requires zero-height cross-character clustering, RMT predicts O(1/T) negligible); (F3) adaptive (q, a) protocol Aggarwal-AP analogue (slot 4). **Predicted slot-2 outcome**: AFE table sharing gives constant-factor speedup at most (analogous to Schönhage Taylor in Thread 5 slot 2); χ-amortisation closes negatively unless RMT correlation can be promoted to algorithmic primitive. **What slot 1 enables**: empirical baseline 22.7ms/query at q=1009, K=200, x=10⁶; an explicit cost-decomposition primitive for slot 2-5 to extend with real (q-batched, χ-batched) Dirichlet AFE sums. **Cross-domain ingredient**: amortised algorithmics (Tarjan 1985 / Demaine-Patrascu 2008) USED I (continuing from Thread 5) — first extension from ζ-zero/x-batched to L-zero/χ-batched at fixed x in the project. Cites E1.5 (per-query bit-content barrier; per-(q, a) bit content ~log(π(x)/φ(q)) per query, a-batched amortisation saves SETUP cost but NOT evaluation), E3.1 (Connes amortisation; same setup-vs-eval decoupling generalised from ζ to L(s, χ)), E6.7 (Deléglise-Rivat per-query Θ(x^{2/3}/log²) — orthogonal pillar), S224 (Correlation Dichotomy template; slot-1's a-batching is the q-axis trivial-direction analogue). See `experiments/analytic/batched_q_amortisation/{per_q_a_explicit_formula_profile.py, per_q_a_explicit_formula_profile_results.md, per_q_a_decoupled_profile.csv, per_q_a_amortisation.csv, per_q_a_sieve_sanity.csv}`, `archive/sessions/session227_commit_p1_per_q_a_profile.md`. | 227 |
    873:| §P.P1 batched-on-modulus q amortisation, slot 3 — end-to-end Dirichlet-L zero finder using FFT-shared full AFE (main + reflected via conjugate-of-main identity) + Hardy Z function + sign-change bracketing + linear-interp refinement: does slot-2's L-eval primitive lift to a ≥3× single-query speedup on end-to-end π(x; q, a) computation? | REFINEMENT (architecture + bounded partial-positive at the FFT-vs-DIRECT axis) | I (slot-2's 5× projection FALSIFIED at 1.01×; FFT primitive structurally load-bearing for parity, slot 4-5 attack composite-q + Riemann-Siegel) | Thread 6 commit step 3/5 (S229). Built `experiments/analytic/batched_q_amortisation/slot3_zero_finder_dirichlet.py` (~470 lines): full AFE main term M(t, χ) via slot-2 FFT primitive `phi · ifft(W_aggregate, axis=0)`; reflected term `W_χ · (q/π)^{-it} · ratio_gamma(t, a) · conj(M)` exploiting `Σ χ̄(n)/n^{1/2-it} = conj(Σ χ(n)/n^{1/2+it})` simplification for prime q (NO additional FFT — pointwise multiply); Gauss sums τ(χ_j) via FFT identity in single length-φ FFT; Hardy Z `Z_χ(t) = e^{i·θ(t)} · L(1/2+it, χ)` with `θ_χ(t) = (t/2)·log(q/π) + arg Γ((1/2+a+it)/2) - arg(W_χ)/2` via `np.imag(scipy.special.loggamma)`; vectorised sign-change bracketing across all (φ, n_t) Z values via `np.where(sign(Z[:,:-1]) * sign(Z[:,1:]) < 0)` + linear-interp refinement (9× speedup over per-character Python loop, project tooling fix). N_AFE oversized 12× (without Riemann-Siegel correction terms, balanced N gives error ~1 absolute breaking sign-change detection). Principal character (j=0) uses cached zeta-zeros directly. **Cross-validation against mpmath ground truth**: q=11, χ_1 first 5 zeros: slot-3 (3.55, 6.61, 7.88, 11.60, 13.31) vs mpmath (3.52, 6.64, 7.86, 11.60, 13.35), max diff 0.033; q=11, χ_5 (Legendre) first 4 zeros agree to 0.06 abs. **End-to-end timing q=1009, K=200, x=10⁶, 3-run median**: t_zerofind 151.5ms, t_partialsum 34.8ms, t_single 186.3ms; **1.01× parity vs slot-1 synthetic baseline (187.7ms)**. zeros[median per char] = 135 (out of K_target=200 — n_t=823 grid limited; not all close-spaced zeros captured). **HEADLINE — FFT vs DIRECT no-FFT-sharing**: q=101 K=200 t_max=198 n_t=849 N_afe=678: FFT 43.4ms vs DIRECT 41.1ms = 0.95× (FFT loses at small q); q=1009 K=200 t_max=153 n_t=823 N_afe=1883: **FFT 173.2ms vs DIRECT 352.9ms = 2.04× FFT speedup** (larger than slot-2's L-eval-only 1.13-1.79× because oversized N_afe=1883 widens FFT advantage at large N where BLAS saturates earlier than FFT). Crossover q ∈ [200, 500]. **Internal cost decomposition q=1009 K=200**: setup 0.3ms, AFE 139.3ms (73%), Hardy Z theta 28.2ms (15%), bracket 22.2ms (12%); AFE step dominates. **Amortised at M=256**: slot-3 FFT 35.4ms vs slot-1 23.4ms (slot-3 0.66× slot-1) — T_full_per_x dominated by partial-sum eval (135 real zeros 34.8ms vs slot-1 200 synthetic zeros 22.7ms), FFT primitive does NOT affect partial-sum eval, **exactly per E1.5 prediction**. **WHY slot-2's "5× single-query speedup" projection was wrong**: slot-1's synthetic generator is already cheap (Newton-on-density inversion vectorised numpy: 0.82 µs/gamma); real Dirichlet-L zeros via FFT-Hardy-Z cost 1.1 µs/gamma — only 1.34× more, NOT slower. Plus Hardy theta + bracket costs ~50ms unaccounted-for. **Structural finding (B-grade): real Dirichlet-L zeros for the price of synthetic density-inversion zeros, via cyclic DFT identity + truncated AFE main+reflected**. Not Correlation-Dichotomy-shaped (33× at M=64) but a non-trivial calibration of slot-2's primitive. **Falsifier list F3.1-F3.4 framing slots 4-5**: F3.1 Riemann-Siegel correction terms drop N_afe by 5-10× (Berry 1995 / Coffey 2003) → may push slot-3 from parity to 3-5× partial-positive; F3.2 composite-q multi-axis FFT for q = p₁p₂ (slot 4 PRIORITY-b); F3.3 cross-conductor batches (slot 5); F3.4 better FFT engines (FFTW3/cuFFT) close BLAS-vs-FFT gap. **Cross-domain ingredient**: Hardy Z function for Dirichlet L (Davenport "Multiplicative NT" §15-19 / Booker "Numerical computation of Riemann zeta function") USED I — first end-to-end Dirichlet-L zero finder via Hardy Z within the project; CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §8 row added. Approximate Functional Equation full+reflected for primitive χ with the conjugate-identity simplification for prime q USED I. scipy.special.loggamma for complex argument (engineering, not new mathematics). Continuation of amortised algorithmics (Tarjan 1985 / Demaine-Patrascu 2008) USED I from Threads 5-6 slot 1-2. Cites E1.5 (per-query bit-content; amortised regime unchanged exactly per E1.5), E2.1 (spectral / sieve interface; cyclic DFT is dual-group spectral primitive), E3.1 (CCM setup-vs-eval decoupling; slot-3 confirms FFT primitive lifts setup but not eval), E6.6 (Aggarwal — orthogonal to slot 3; cross-call vs within-call amortisation), S224 (Correlation Dichotomy template; slot 3 produces real-zeros-for-synthetic-cost shape, smaller than 33× pattern), S227 (slot 1 baseline matched at 1.01×), S228 (slot 2 primitive propagated end-to-end with quantitative falsification of 5× projection). See `experiments/analytic/batched_q_amortisation/{slot3_zero_finder_dirichlet.py, slot3_zero_finder_dirichlet_results.md, slot3_end_to_end.csv, slot3_fft_vs_direct.csv}`, `archive/sessions/session229_commit_p1_dirichlet_zero_finder.md`. | 229 |
    874:| §P.P1 batched-on-modulus q amortisation, slot 2 — AFE-shared L-value evaluation across characters mod q via cyclic DFT identity over (Z/qZ)*: does sharing the `1/n^{1/2+it}` table across all chi mod q give meaningful per-character cost reduction over optimised direct BLAS matmul? | REFINEMENT (operational confirmation + bounded partial-positive) | I (slot-2 falsifier α confirmed; wall-clock benefit bounded constant; slot 3-5 attack adjacent angles) | Thread 6 commit step 2/5 (S228). Built `experiments/analytic/batched_q_amortisation/slot2_afe_shared_l_eval.py` — three implementations of truncated AFE main term `L(1/2+it, chi) ≈ Σ_{n ≤ N, gcd(n,q)=1} chi(n) / n^{1/2+it}`: DIRECT (`chi_at_n @ cp` via vectorised `char_table_at_residues` building only φ × N_co columns); AGGREGATE_MATMUL (build W[r, j] aggregate then `char_table @ W` of shape φ × φ — strictly worse than DIRECT at N_co < φ, skipped at q ≥ 5000 due to φ² memory); FFT (aggregate W in log-g order, then `phi · ifft(W, axis=0)` exploits cyclic DFT identity `χ_j(g^k) = ω^{jk}` to compute L for ALL φ chars at once per t). **Cross-method correctness**: max relative error vs DIRECT is ~1e-15 across q ∈ {11, 31, 101} (numerical floating-point limit). **HONEST cost profile** (12 rows q ∈ {101, 1009, 10007} × t_max ∈ {50, 200} × n_t ∈ {400, 800}): FFT wins in 11/12 configurations with median speedup 1.7× over OPTIMISED DIRECT, range 0.74×-6.35×. **Scaling profile** (q ∈ {101, 251, 503, 1009, 2003, 5003}, t_max=100, n_t=400): empirical FFT speedup is ESSENTIALLY FLAT in q at ~1.7×, while theoretical FLOP-ratio `N_co/log φ` grows from 5.8× (q=101) to 22.9× (q=5003). **Gap = BLAS hardware FLOP rate (~30 GFLOP/s, well-optimised) vs np.fft Bluestein (~3-5 GFLOP/s) — ratio ~8-12×**. End-to-end pi(x; q, a) implication: single-query speedup ~5× vs slot-1 synthetic baseline (188ms → ~33ms at q=1009, K=200, x=10⁶), but amortised regime UNCHANGED at slot-1 T_full_per_x = 22.7ms (set by phi(q) · K · 50ns partial-sum eval, which slot-2 primitive does NOT affect). **NOT a Correlation-Dichotomy-shaped partial-positive** (not the 33×-shaped result Thread 5 produced). Slot-1 falsifier (α) is structurally CORRECT but practically WEAK: cyclic DFT identity gives the right algebraic primitive, but BLAS hardware efficiency closes the wall-clock gap. **Methodological correction**: replaced slot-1-inherited `all_characters_table()` (φ² Python double loop, O(100s) overhead at q=10007) with vectorised `char_table_at_residues(q, residues)` (np.exp over outer-product (φ, len(residues))). Affects all future χ-side experiments. **Falsifier list F2.1-F2.4 framing slot 3+**: F2.1 alternate FFT impls (FFTW3 / cuFFT) may close BLAS-vs-FFT gap; F2.2 propagation to end-to-end zero-finding (slot 3 PRIORITY a); F2.3 composite-q via multi-axis FFT (slot 3 PRIORITY b); F2.4 better-tuned BLAS may widen the gap further. **Cross-domain ingredient**: FFT over the dual group of (Z/qZ)* (Bober-Hiary 2017 / Booker-Lobb 2009) USED I — first time in the project; CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §10 row "FFT over Dirichlet character group" PROPOSED → USED I (constant-factor speedup confirmed but not sub-linear amortisation). Continuation of amortised algorithmics (Tarjan 1985 / Demaine-Patrascu 2008) USED I from Thread 5, now applied at the χ-axis with the cyclic DFT primitive. Cites E1.5 (per-query bit-content; amortised regime unchanged exactly per E1.5), E3.1 (Connes setup-vs-eval decoupling; slot-2 attacks setup), E2.1 (spectral / dual-group structure of (Z/qZ)*), S224 (Correlation Dichotomy template — slot-2 implements the χ-axis analogue with bounded wall-clock benefit), S227 (slot-1 baseline directly extended). See `experiments/analytic/batched_q_amortisation/{slot2_afe_shared_l_eval.py, slot2_afe_shared_l_eval_results.md, slot2_l_eval_profile.csv, slot2_scaling.csv, slot2_scaling_quick.csv}`, `archive/sessions/session228_commit_p1_afe_shared_l_eval.md`. | 228 |
