. The HL imprint and SL_2(Z) Möbius action on Q (which changes denominators non-multiplicatively) are categorically incompatible; chi_P does NOT carry quantum-modular structure for any weight k or any SL_2(Z) generator with c ≠ 0. **Adds EDGE E7.22** (negative-shape) — first identification of HL/SL_2(Z) structural incompatibility. **A-grade ruled out**: no quantum-modular polylog primality witness exists from rational evaluation of f_N. **Successor B-grade target identified**: Eichler integral of half-integer-weight pseudo-modular form with prime-supported Fourier coefficients (Bringmann-Folsom-Ono-Rhoades 2017 §21.3) — does the lift to upper half-plane H carry quantum-modular structure that rational evaluation misses? **Cross-domain ingredient**: Zagier 2010 quantum modular forms (Clay Math. Proc. 11) — UNUSED before; CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §6 row PROPOSED → USED I (E7.22). Cites E2.21 (S138, HL imprint at L^∞), E2.20 (S134, Mahler), E2.13 (Gowers U^k), E1.5 (explicit formula), E7.21 (S197, natural boundary). See `experiments/analytic/d37_quantum_modular_chi_p/{d37_quantum_modular_chi_p.py, d37_quantum_modular_chi_p_results.md, summary_N*.json, rows_k*_N*.json}`, `archive/sessions/session225_d37_quantum_modular_chi_p.md`. | 225 |
    871	| §D.D11 — Shadow-tomography sample / query complexity for π(x) extraction (Aaronson 2018; Huang-Kueng-Preskill 2020 classical shadows): under the random-Rademacher (= random-Pauli-Z) classical-shadow query model y_j := ⟨σ_j, χ_P⟩ with σ_j ~ Unif({±1}^N), how many queries K suffice to estimate π(M) for ALL M ∈ [1, N] simultaneously to ε accuracy? | E (cross-domain HKP technique survives import; cumulative-window observable is structurally global so polylog speedup is absent) | E | S226 cross-domain attack mode (D11 picked by run.sh dispatcher prompt). Built `experiments/information_theory/shadow_tomography_chi_p.py` — implements random-Rademacher classical-shadow estimator and runs K-sweep at N = 2¹⁵ = 32 768. **Theorem 1 (closed form)**: linear unbiased estimator π̂(M; K) = (1/K) Σ_j y_j · ⟨σ_j, 1_{[1, M]}⟩ is unbiased with Var[π̂(M; K)] = (M·π(N) − π(M) + π(M)² − π(M)) / K ~ M·π(N)/K (leading order). Proof: expand four-σ moment; under n ≠ m, n' ≠ m' constraints only pairings (B) n=n', m=m' and (C) n=m', m=n' contribute; B sum = M·π(N) − π(M), C sum = π(M)² − π(M). **Corollary (query lower bound)**: for ε-accuracy max over dyadic M of abs(π̂(M) − π(M)) ≤ ε with probability ≥ 1−δ across all log₂N dyadic windows (Chebyshev + union), K ≥ Ω(N · π(N) · log(N/δ) / ε²) = Ω̃(N²/log²N · ε^{-2}). For ε=1, K = Θ̃(N²) — strictly polynomial in N, NOT polylog. Direct sieving solves the same task in O(N log log N) ops; shadow oracle is asymptotically WORSE than direct sieving by factor π(N) / (log²N · log log N). **A-grade target (polylog QUERY complexity for π) RULED OUT** within classical-shadow framework. **Mechanism**: cumulative-window indicator 1_{[1, M]} is a *global observable* in the random-Rademacher ensemble; HKP §3 shadow-norm bound ‖O‖²_shadow ≤ 4^k · ‖O‖∞² for k-body local observables makes cumulative windows have shadow norm Θ(M) — exponential in effective Pauli weight. Combined with ‖χ_P‖² = π(N) matches Var = M·π(N)/K exactly. **Empirical verification** at N = 2¹⁵, K ∈ {10², 3·10², 10³, 3·10³, 10⁴, 3·10⁴, 10⁵} with n_trials ∈ {5, ..., 20}: empirical std at every (K, M) within ±30% of theoretical (within finite-trial Monte Carlo noise); L∞ error scales as K^{−0.50} matching CLT prediction; extrapolated K_* for L∞ ≤ 1 satisfies K_*/(N·π(N)) ≈ 1. **Adds EDGE E1.11** — random-Rademacher shadow query complexity of π(M) is Θ̃(N · π(M)) per simultaneous-M target. **Third computational axis** (random-mask oracle / sample complexity) the project has explored, orthogonal to E1.5 (info rate), E5.x (circuit size), E6.x (time / space). **Distinct from**: E1.5 (incremental per-step entropy of π mod m — adaptive single-modulus chain; this bounds non-adaptive batch query across all M); E5.6 (deterministic non-uniform circuit size; this is randomised query); E6.6 (Aggarwal adaptive binary-search with EXACT π queries; this is non-adaptive random-mask only); E6.7 (Meissel-Lehmer time-space deterministic). **No reshuffling** of orthogonal random ensembles (Rademacher → Gaussian → Walsh-Hadamard) changes the Θ(M) shadow norm of cumulative-window indicators. **Successor entries** (added inline as §D.D11.a/b/c in ATTACK_VECTORS.md): D11.a Walsh-Hadamard structured shadow (predicted closure, M²/K residual scaling forces K_WH = Ω(N)); D11.b Möbius / Liouville shadow (potentially conditional A-grade under RH via L∞ residual O(M^{1/2 + ε}) of Σ λ); D11.c Bernoulli-iid density-matched control (B-grade duplicate of theorem). **Cross-domain ingredient**: HKP 2020 *Nature Physics* 16, 1050 = arXiv:2002.08953; Aaronson 2018 STOC = arXiv:1711.01053. CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §8 row "Holographic / shadow tomography" promoted PROPOSED → USED-E. Cites E1.5 (info-content axis), E2.21 (parity major-arc Walsh-Hadamard L∞ — D11.a successor), E5.6 (circuit size), E6.6 (Aggarwal adaptive). **Channelled mathematician**: Aaronson — sample / query complexity in randomised computational models; framing in his style; answer (Ω̃(N²) — strictly polynomial) shows 